  challenged assumption: the signed-packet vertices do not have to be runners.
    This quotient preserves the far-peel discrepancy and additive model class;
    it destroys the full endpoint state word, so it must feed back into HYP-2648/2675.

Proof-route reading
  1. Same-sign packet rows are not random ET errors; they live in compressed additive models.
  2. Ruzsa modeling should split low-K rows into finite cyclic/GAP models before scalar bounds.
  3. Plunnecke-Ruzsa growth is useful in the complementary branch: larger growth spreads endpoints,
     making the signed sector packet a cancellation problem rather than a same-sign pocket.
  4. Landau/Chebyshev/Bertrand analogy: local packet bias can persist on a finite scale,
     but the scale ledger asks for a reset in each dyadic block after the finite resonance.
  5. No LRC(14) proof is claimed here; this turns the next obligation into a finite-model
     same-sign classification plus a signed ET packet bound on high-growth rows.
PASS: signed packet/Ruzsa scout complete.
